From 9a9494794fbeecefb8e4e6320cd8c2174b16fd4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 20:18:51 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] server: TENSORFOLD_TOKEN_CACHE_MB (rank 0 only, off by
 default) caches the tokenizer's plain pieces between the added-token split
 and the encoder, and the done line carries render_s and encode_s

Step 0 of docs/baseten-ultrafast-plan.md item 1, in the same patch: a request's
template render and encode are timed and printed on its done line as render_s
and encode_s, after every existing field (0070's timings, then 0117's energy
suffix), so parsers of the old line still match. Unconditional, three monotonic
clock reads per request (render start, render end, encode end); a completion
prompt of raw text has render_s 0 and its own encode, a prompt of ids has both
0. The figures are the request's own, not a rolling average.

The cache: with TENSORFOLD_TOKEN_CACHE_MB=N the server allocates an LRU of N MiB
and Tokenizer.encodeWith consults it for each plain piece the added-token split
hands over, keyed on that piece's bytes with 0028's marks kept (a marked and an
unmarked spelling never share an entry) and holding its ids. Entries cost
len(text)+4*ids bytes; a piece larger than a quarter of the cap is not cached;
when the cap would be passed the older half by last use goes. One mutex around
the map, held for a lookup or an insert only, never during an encode (prepares
run concurrently under prepare_gate). Unset, blank, 0 or anything that is not a
count leaves the cache off: no allocation, no map, and HfText.encode calls
Tokenizer.encode exactly as before. Suggested first value 32.

The switch is rank 0's only: HfText (the tokenizer behind rank 0's HTTP server)
reads it, and rank 1 runs no tokenizer, so the two ranks have nothing to
compare at start.

Independence: this tokenizer hands each plain piece to the normalizer,
pre-tokenizer and BPE on its own (Walk), and splitAdded has already applied an
added token's lstrip/rstrip to that token's own span, so a piece's ids depend on
its neighbours only through `first` (Metaspace's "first" scheme, ByteLevel's
add_prefix_space): the text's first piece is encoded plainly and never cached,
and every other piece is cached on the bytes it is given. single_word is refused
at load. No parallel encode of misses; images are expanded after encoding
(prompt.zig spans), so they need no special case.

Tests: zig build test passes and now runs the tokenizer module's own tests
(build/cuda.zig adds it to hostTests; before, none of that module's tests ran in
either test command): cache.zig's switch parse ("32"/" 32 " on; unset, "", 0,
junk and negatives off), the cap/eviction bound and the quarter-cap skip, and
tokenizer.zig's cached equals plain encode on a cold and a warm cache over
prose, every added token, 0028's marks, pieces of exactly 255/256/257 bytes,
empty pieces, adjacent specials and non-UTF-8 bytes; the first piece not cached
and a piece after an added token cached; eviction holding the bound with results
still plain; four threads on the same long text giving the plain ids; and the
switch off consulting no cache. zig/tests/server/zig_test.sh passes too (186
server tests, including HfText.encode's cache wiring and its off path). Not run
on a pair and not benchmarked: this is the implementation only; step 0's numbers
come from the new render_s/encode_s fields.
